In article <25328@cs.yale.edu> beigel-richard@CS.YALE.EDU (Richard Beigel) writes:
:Beginning and intermediate players stay on their toes because the
:monsters are so nasty.  Really high-level characters (read 21st level
:with AC -17, +4 Firebrand, instrinsic resist everything, teleport
:ability, 250HP, and maybe a few things I should not mention) can
:become very complacent, because they can beat the tar out of any
:monster in the dungeon.  If they get really unlucky in battle, they
:can usually teleport away and recover.  The possibility of sudden
:death adds an element of realistic fear to the game.
     True enough, but as I've said, this is skill, not strategy.  I
don't think this adds much to the game.  I'd rather see harder monsters.

:(1) To make the game more forgiving of obvious blunders that could be
:caused by mistyping.
:
:(2) To make the game hard and scary so that players will keep on their
:toes.
:
:Solutions proposed to (1):
:
:    (a) add noblunder-type options
:    (b) flush typeahead in certain situations
     No reason why we should do it only in certain situations.
     At first I thought, "What a non-intuitive thing to do!"  But the
game already flushes the typeahead when you get a "--more--".  I don't
see the problem in that regard.  (Of course, neither has anyone
mentioned it, but whatever.)

:Obviously I prefer (a).  I think that (b) would be helpful in many
:situations.  Rogue flushed typeahead in battle.  This saved my
:rogueskin on numerous occasions.  I don't know if the flush applied to
:the first hit though, since you weren't yet in battle; the first hit
:is the deadly one with floating eyes.  And what about pools?  Should
:we flush typeahead whenever the player is next to water?  I am not
:sure I would like that.  
     Just do it in all cases.  No problems.

:Also, I doubt that flushing typeahead would
:work well over a modem or telnet connection, since the typeahead might
:be at the other end of the line; this is the most serious defect, I
:think, in option (b).  
     Doesn't really exist over a modem, as long as both sender and
receiver can transmit/receive at the baud rate (i.e. baud rate is the
limiting factor, not CPU speed.  Almost always the case.)

:Solutions to (2):
:
:    (a) don't add noblunder option -- this keeps the game scary
:    (b) make the game harder in other ways
:
:I think that (b) would be more fun for everyone -- even if the
:noblunder-type options are not added.  Let's give players something
:*really* to be scared about.  You've heard some of my ideas: slippery
:rocks next to pools, moat monsters that can pull you under, ogres that
:can push you in.  Then even careful players could enjoy abject
:terror.  How about letting floating eyes attack you from a distance?
:I have never understood why they only attack you when you swing at
:them.  If I stand right next to an eye and throw darts at it, it has
:no counterattack.  Why not?  I'm looking at it and I'm within its
:range.  If I was several squares away I can imagine that its gaze
:would be less effective, but I still have to look at it in order to
:throw missile weapons.  If this is changed, then game balance would
:have to be carefully considered on the low levels.
     Personally, I think a lot of thought would have to go into it
first.  How fun would a game be if you entered a room and were
immediately frozen by a floating eye?  If you throw at it from right
next to it, I don't have a problem, however.  Most sudden death
features, I believe, should introduce a *strategy* component rather than
a skill component.  There's also the "first time" factor.  I can just
imagine the frustration of a player who makes it to the castle for the
first time and dies for a reason he could hardly have predicted. 
Meanwhile, slippery rocks present simply the same danger as pools for
someone who's died that way, and you don't even get the visual cue this
way.  I don't see the strategy component.

